The Proxy design pattern is a structural pattern that provides a surrogate or
 placeholder object to control access to another object, called the real subject.
 Instead of interacting directly with the real object, clients interact with the proxy,
 which can add extra functionality before or after forwarding the request to the real object.


 Components
 Subject (Interface): Defines the common interface for the real object and proxy.

 Real Subject: The actual object that performs the work.

 Proxy: Implements the same interface as the real subject; controls access and may add additional behaviors such as lazy initialization, access control, caching, or logging.


 Ex : Virtual Proxy for Image Loading in Java